    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Local winds are generated due to localized spatial differences in temperature, atmospheric pressure, and regional topography.",
        "Major hot local winds include Chinook (North America), Foehn (Alps), Harmattan (West Africa), Loo (Northern India), and Sirocco (Sahara).",
        "Major cold local winds include Mistral (France), Bora (Adriatic Coast), Pampero (South America), and Blizzard (Polar/Tundra regions).",
        "Sirocco carries red dust from the Sahara across the Mediterranean, causing the phenomenon of 'Blood Rain' in Southern Europe.",
        "Harmattan is termed 'The Doctor' because its dry nature brings relief from the damp humidity of the Gulf of Guinea coast."
      ],
      "id": "9v1j2f3"
    },
    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "Matching local wind names with their specific origin regions (e.g., Chinook - Rockies, Foehn - Alps, Harmattan - West Africa) is frequently tested in UPSC and SSC exams.",
        "'Blood Rain' in Southern Italy is caused by Saharan desert dust transported by the Sirocco wind.",
        "Loo is an extremely hot, dry wind blowing across the Indo-Gangetic Plains during May and June."
      ],
